WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.400
Picture an office building.

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.414
Small offices, one team in each.

3
00:00:06.414 --> 00:00:10.443
All day, people walk the corridors with requests for each other.

4
00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:12.533
Each team has its own habits.

5
00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:15.171
One knocks five times, then knocks again.

6
00:00:15.171 --> 00:00:17.359
Another gives up after one second.

7
00:00:17.359 --> 00:00:19.483
Nobody checks who is at the door.

8
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.095
One afternoon, payroll slows down.

9
00:00:22.095 --> 00:00:24.279
A queue forms outside its door.

10
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.104
The people stuck there are late for everyone waiting on them.

11
00:00:28.104 --> 00:00:29.700
Soon those queues grow too.

12
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.245
By four o'clock, half the building is standing in corridors.

13
00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:35.362
No team did anything foolish.

14
00:00:35.362 --> 00:00:38.304
Each habit made sense from inside one office.

15
00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:42.553
The trouble lives between the offices, where nobody is in charge.

16
00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:49.143
Services, separate programs that call each other over the network, have exactly this building.

17
00:00:49.143 --> 00:00:52.831
Before one call is safe, somebody has to decide all of this.

18
00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:55.884
None of it is the work the service exists to do.

19
00:00:55.884 --> 00:00:59.329
And left alone, every team solves it a little differently.

20
00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:05.149
Those are the office habits, and they surface during an outage, when they are hardest to untangle.

21
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:10.104
Wouldn't it be nice if the door itself handled it, the same way for everyone?

22
00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:13.083
So put a receptionist at the door.

23
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:14.790
The app makes a plain call.

24
00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:18.015
The receptionist makes the real call on its behalf.

25
00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:23.675
The receptionist adds the time limit, the second try, the badge check and the counting.

26
00:01:23.675 --> 00:01:25.927
One set of habits, for every office.

27
00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:28.879
A program that does this is called a proxy.

28
00:01:28.879 --> 00:01:30.603
The one we'll use is Envoy.

29
00:01:30.603 --> 00:01:35.903
And a proxy that lives right next to the app, in the same Pod, is called a sidecar.

30
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:39.419
Here's the nice part: the app doesn't change at all.

31
00:01:39.419 --> 00:01:44.285
Packet rules inside the Pod quietly hand every connection to the proxy first.

32
00:01:44.285 --> 00:01:47.887
The app keeps calling the same address and never notices.

33
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.273
Let's build the smallest version.

34
00:01:51.273 --> 00:01:57.744
Our lab app, hello-devops, a tiny web app that greets every visitor, runs as three copies.

35
00:01:57.744 --> 00:01:59.757
v1a and v1b are version one.

36
00:01:59.967 --> 00:02:04.944
v2 is a canary, a new version that gets a small share of traffic first.

37
00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:11.616
Envoy's route sends 90 out of every 100 requests to version one, and 10 to the canary.

38
00:02:11.833 --> 00:02:13.515
We send 200 requests.

39
00:02:13.515 --> 00:02:15.756
How many land on the canary?

40
00:02:15.756 --> 00:02:17.037
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:20.267 --> 00:02:23.067
About twenty, if it split perfectly.

42
00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:24.856
The lab got 175 and 25.

43
00:02:24.856 --> 00:02:31.700
Envoy picks at random for each request, so the numbers wander a little around 90 and 10.

44
00:02:32.333 --> 00:02:35.092
Now let's break one copy on purpose.

45
00:02:35.092 --> 00:02:39.766
v1b keeps its visit count in its own Redis, a small database.

46
00:02:39.766 --> 00:02:40.609
We stop it.

47
00:02:40.833 --> 00:02:44.191
Now real visits to v1b fail with error 500.

48
00:02:44.191 --> 00:02:49.188
But its health page, slash healthz, still answers 200, all fine.

49
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:54.218
The obvious hope: Envoy runs its own health check, every five seconds.

50
00:02:54.218 --> 00:02:55.457
Surely it notices.

51
00:02:55.457 --> 00:02:58.072
Let's be fair; that check really runs.

52
00:02:58.072 --> 00:02:59.517
And it keeps passing.

53
00:02:59.733 --> 00:03:03.923
An office with the door wide open, and a phone nobody answers.

54
00:03:03.923 --> 00:03:06.829
A health check only asks: is the door open?

55
00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:09.058
So we send twenty requests.

56
00:03:09.058 --> 00:03:10.483
What does curl see?

57
00:03:10.483 --> 00:03:11.683
Pause and guess.

58
00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.151
Twenty times 200.

59
00:03:16.151 --> 00:03:18.433
Three requests did fail on v1b.

60
00:03:18.433 --> 00:03:26.088
But the route has a retry policy, and each retry goes to a different host than the one that just failed.

61
00:03:26.088 --> 00:03:27.855
So v1a answered instead.

62
00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:33.069
And after the third error in a row, Envoy took v1b out of the pool.

63
00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:38.193
Its admin page says so: three retries, three successes, one host ejected.

64
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.131
That is outlier detection.

65
00:03:40.131 --> 00:03:43.860
Instead of asking the door, it watches the real answers.

66
00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:50.452
Three 500s in a row, and the host is skipped for thirty seconds, longer each time it happens again.

67
00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:53.027
Two ways to notice a sick host, then.

68
00:03:53.027 --> 00:03:55.197
A health check knocks on the door.

69
00:03:55.197 --> 00:03:58.068
Outlier detection listens to the phone calls.

70
00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:00.588
Now the guardrail.

71
00:04:00.588 --> 00:04:02.162
Retries hide failures.

72
00:04:02.162 --> 00:04:04.739
That is their use, and their danger.

73
00:04:04.967 --> 00:04:08.616
Picture a chain: A calls B, B calls C, C calls D.

74
00:04:08.616 --> 00:04:11.967
Every hop tries once, and then retries twice.

75
00:04:11.967 --> 00:04:13.456
Three tries per hop.

76
00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:18.111
When D is struggling, one user request becomes three calls to B.

77
00:04:18.111 --> 00:04:18.806
Nine to C.

78
00:04:18.806 --> 00:04:22.625
And twenty-seven to D, just when D can least afford it.

79
00:04:22.833 --> 00:04:25.313
The kind receptionist turned into a mob.

80
00:04:25.313 --> 00:04:30.088
So keep retries few and short, and only for requests that are safe to repeat.

81
00:04:30.088 --> 00:04:34.800
The lab's route waits three seconds in total, and gives each try one second.

82
00:04:35.633 --> 00:04:37.711
And remember the payroll queue?

83
00:04:37.711 --> 00:04:42.537
Envoy's circuit breakers are limits on connections and waiting requests.

84
00:04:42.537 --> 00:04:48.837
Past a limit, it answers 503, service unavailable, at once, instead of letting the queue grow.

85
00:04:49.067 --> 00:04:50.618
None of this is free.

86
00:04:50.618 --> 00:04:55.715
Every call takes one extra hop, and every proxy costs CPU and memory.

87
00:04:56.533 --> 00:04:58.158
So the moral of the story.

88
00:04:58.158 --> 00:05:00.533
The trouble lives between the offices.

89
00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:07.220
Put the habits at the door, once, the same for everyone, and let the receptionist watch who really answers.

90
00:05:07.433 --> 00:05:08.523
A quick check.

91
00:05:08.523 --> 00:05:13.035
A host's health page says 200, but its real requests fail.

92
00:05:13.035 --> 00:05:14.514
Which tool notices?

93
00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:15.759
Pause and think.

94
00:05:18.967 --> 00:05:20.270
Outlier detection.

95
00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:23.311
It watches the real answers, not the door.

96
00:05:23.533 --> 00:05:27.551
Next, we hand the receptionists to a building manager, Istio.

97
00:05:27.551 --> 00:05:33.019
How does every proxy get its rules and its ID badge, without anyone writing a file?
